  "hash": "c8decf2c073d329054945da4cbbbf7aa",
  "result": {
    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Midterm 1 Practice Problems\"\nformat: \n  html:\n    css: [webex.css]\n    include-after-body: [webex.js]\n    embed-resources: true \n---\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAs a study note, you should spend just as much time *understanding* the topics as you do *solving* the questions below.\n\nIt is **strongly** recommended that you write your full solutions on paper for each question **before** looking at the solutions. This will help you understand the concepts better and will also help you if you need to refer to your notes later. \n\n::: {.callout-important}\n### Don't shortcut your learning!\n\nYou can take a forklift to the gym and lift all the weights, but it doesn't mean you got any stronger. You can read and understand the solutions, but it doesn't mean you got any smarter. \n\nYou gain muscle by lifting weights that are near the limit of what you can handle - too heavy and you'll just fail, too light and you won't gain muscle. You gain knowledge in a similar way. In both cases, it should be a *managable struggle*!!!\n\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n1.  In a histogram, how are the \"bins\" usually created? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_KUVGJGZDGF'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KUVGJGZDGF\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Where there are more data, more bins are created.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KUVGJGZDGF\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Bins are always 1 unit wide.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KUVGJGZDGF\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Bin is short for binary and has nothing to do with visualization.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KUVGJGZDGF\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>Bins are chosen as equal lengths across the range of the data.</span></label></div>\n2.  Which of the following best describes a violin plot? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_OJDIXFDHZK'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_OJDIXFDHZK\" value=\"\"></input> <span>A bigger version of a viola plot.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_OJDIXFDHZK\" value=\"\"></input> <span>A plot of measurements taken from violins.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_OJDIXFDHZK\" value=\"\"></input> <span>A better version of a histogram.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_OJDIXFDHZK\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>A sideways density plot, mirrored across the axis.</span></label></div>\n3.  Which of the following plots would allow a comparison between two categorical variables? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_KTNSLHFPWK'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KTNSLHFPWK\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>Bar plots</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KTNSLHFPWK\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Histograms</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KTNSLHFPWK\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Violin plots</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KTNSLHFPWK\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Box plots</span></label></div>\n\n\n| id|name  | height| year| grade|\n|--:|:-----|------:|----:|-----:|\n|  1|Bonk  |    1.2|    1|    56|\n|  2|Lilah |    0.4|   NA|    NA|\n|  3|June  |    1.4|    3|    88|\n|  4|Judy  |    1.6|    2|    75|\n|  5|Julie |    1.7|    1|    72|\n|  6|Jodie |    1.9|    1|    83|\n|  7|Jill  |    1.4|    2|    81|\n\n4.  Consider the data above. Which of the following plots would provide meaningful information? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_AKMIEVFYKG'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_AKMIEVFYKG\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>A boxplot of grade versus year</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_AKMIEVFYKG\" value=\"\"></input> <span>A scatterplot of height versus grade</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_AKMIEVFYKG\" value=\"\"></input> <span>A bar plot of ID versus Name</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_AKMIEVFYKG\" value=\"\"></input> <span>A bar plot of height versus grade</span></label></div>\n5.  Suppose we have a data set named \"`sales`\" with columns `product`, `volume`, and `city`, containing the name of the product and the number of that product that was sold in each city. Fill in the blanks for the following plotting code. The resulting plot is shown below.\n\n `ggplot(` <input class='webex-solveme nospaces' size='5' data-answer='[\"sales\"]'/> `) + aes(x = ` <input class='webex-solveme nospaces' size='4' data-answer='[\"city\"]'/> `, y = ` <input class='webex-solveme nospaces' size='6' data-answer='[\"volume\"]'/> `, fill = ` <input class='webex-solveme nospaces' size='7' data-answer='[\"product\"]'/> `) + geom_col(position=\"dodge\")`![](MT1_Practice_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-2-1.png){width=672}\n\n| student_number|assessment | grade|\n|--------------:|:----------|-----:|\n|      123456789|A1         |    90|\n|      123456789|A2         |    83|\n|      123456789|A3         |    74|\n|      123456789|MT         |    89|\n|      124624353|A1         |    60|\n|      124624353|A2         |    65|\n|      124624353|A3         |    70|\n|      124624353|MT         |    76|\n|      984367234|A1         |    73|\n|      984367234|A2         |    74|\n|      984367234|A3         |    76|\n|      984367234|MT         |    71|\n\n6.   Consider the data above. What kind of variable is your student number, and what kind of plot might it be useful? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_MTXBTDRRUO'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_MTXBTDRRUO\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Quantitative discrete, bar plot with heights defined by grade</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_MTXBTDRRUO\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Quantitative discrete, scatter plot of grade versus assessment</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_MTXBTDRRUO\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>Unordered qualitative, bar plot with heights defined by grade</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_MTXBTDRRUO\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Unordered qualitative, scatter plot of grade versus assessment</span></label></div>\n\n\n<div class='webex-solution'><button>Explanation</button>\n\n\nYour student number is just a stand-in for your name. Numbers are assigned (mostly) randomly and have no order. They're names made of digits, not numbers! You can't add or multiply them.\n\n\n</div>\n\n\n![](MT1_Practice_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-3-1.png){width=672}\n\n7.  What's the take-away message from the plot above (labelled Figure 2)? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_XBMIDPGKRP'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_XBMIDPGKRP\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The blue team always wins.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_XBMIDPGKRP\" value=\"\"></input> <span>If you score 4 goals, you&apos;re guaranteed to win.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_XBMIDPGKRP\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Scoring more goals than the other team only has a slight correlation with winning the game.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_XBMIDPGKRP\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>Taking more shots has a very slight correlation with scoring more goals.</span></label></div>\n8.  Figure 2 used veritcal jittering for the number of goals. Why? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_LNMQJZGTRA'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_LNMQJZGTRA\" value=\"\"></input> <span>for display purposes.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_LNMQJZGTRA\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The jittering is done because goals is a continuous variable but was only measured to the nearest whole number; jittering makes it continuous rather than discrete.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_LNMQJZGTRA\" value=\"\"></input> <span>This jittering is inapproriate - it implies that there were games with 4.2 goals which is impossible.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_LNMQJZGTRA\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>Since goals are whole numbers, all games with, say, 20 shots and 4 goals will be plotted in the exact same spot. Jittering allows us to see this.</span></label></div>\n9.  From the plot above (Figure 2), how many shots do you need to take in order to have more than a 50% chance to win the game? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_VDTDMHLPVH'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_VDTDMHLPVH\" value=\"\"></input> <span>20</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_VDTDMHLPVH\" value=\"\"></input> <span>40</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_VDTDMHLPVH\" value=\"\"></input> <span>60</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_VDTDMHLPVH\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>It is impossible to tell from this plot.</span></label></div>\n\nIn Figure 2, does it appear that shooting more makes you more likely to win?\n\n\n<div class='webex-solution'><button>Answer</button>\n\n\nNot really! As you take more shots you get more goals, but perhaps your opponent shoots more as well. If you look at, say, 20 shots in a game, it looks like slightly more than half of the games were won. If you look at 40 shots, it still looks like slightly more than half of the games were won.\n\nIt really matters what the opponent does!\n\n\n</div>\n\n\n![](MT1_Practice_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-4-1.png){width=672}\n\n10.  What is the interpretation of the plot above (Figure 3)? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_RDYAEATDHC'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_RDYAEATDHC\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>The number of shots is uncorrelated with whether the team wins - it&apos;s still a 50-50 chance. There&apos;s more variation at the extremes because there were fewer games with that number of shots.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_RDYAEATDHC\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The overall chance of winning is *not* exactly 50% because very few of the values are actually 50%.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_RDYAEATDHC\" value=\"\"></input> <span>This plot shows how many games a team won at each number of shots.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_RDYAEATDHC\" value=\"\"></input> <span>If a team takes 60 shots, they are guaranteed to lose, but if they take exactly 55 shots they have a 75% chance of winning. During a game, a team should stop shooting when they hit 55 shots.</span></label></div>\n11.  A survey asks respondents for their country, and answers come back as \"Canada\", \"canada\", and \"CAN\". Why do we need to clean these entries before creating a summary chart? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_JGXPGGJVYY'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JGXPGGJVYY\" value=\"\"></input> <span>It changes the numbers from integers to decimals.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JGXPGGJVYY\" value=\"\"></input> <span>R will automatically delete all responses that aren&apos;t spelled correctly.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JGXPGGJVYY\" value=\"\"></input> <span>R will automatically throw an error and refuse to make the plot</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JGXPGGJVYY\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>The computer treats them as 3 completely separate responses.</span></label></div>\n12.  You want to compare the relative proportions of car classes across different drive types (drv), ensuring that every bar reaches 100% height regardless of the total count in each drive category. Which position adjustment should you use in geom_bar()? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_TESMZPPKTE'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_TESMZPPKTE\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>position = \"fill\"</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_TESMZPPKTE\" value=\"\"></input> <span>position = \"dodge\"</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_TESMZPPKTE\" value=\"\"></input> <span>position = \"stack\"</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_TESMZPPKTE\" value=\"\"></input> <span>position = \"identity\"</span></label></div>\n13.  I want to examine the distribution of a single continuous variable (hwy mileage) to check for bimodality and skewness across the entire dataset. Which geometric object is best suited for this task?  <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_KOQEILTJYH'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KOQEILTJYH\" value=\"\"></input> <span>geom_bar()</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KOQEILTJYH\" value=\"\"></input> <span>geom_boxplot()</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KOQEILTJYH\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>geom_histogram()</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_KOQEILTJYH\" value=\"\"></input> <span>geom_point()</span></label></div>\n14.  You plot City Mileage (cty) vs. Highway Mileage (hwy) for 234 cars using geom_point(). When you look at the plot, you only see about 100 dots. What is happening, and why is this plot misleading? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_WYHNQYOSZN'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_WYHNQYOSZN\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>Multiple cars have identical rounded mileage values, causing their points to land on exact same coordinates and stack directly on top of each other.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_WYHNQYOSZN\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Over half of the cars in the dataset have missing mileage values.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_WYHNQYOSZN\" value=\"\"></input> <span>geom_point() only plots a random sample of the data when datasets exceed 100 rows.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_WYHNQYOSZN\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Cars with identical highway and city mileage automatically cancel each other out on the graph.</span></label></div>\n15.  I created a histogram of continuous highway gas mileage (hwy) using geom_histogram(). By default, ggplot2 uses 30 bins and displays a warning suggesting that the user pick a better binwidth. What happens to the story the plot tells if the student sets an extremely small bin width (e.g., binwidth = 0.1) versus an extremely large bin width (e.g., binwidth = 20)? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_TAUPBAIZMC'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_TAUPBAIZMC\" value=\"\"></input> <span>An extremely small bin width converts the plot into a boxplot; an extremely large bin width converts it into a scatterplot.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_TAUPBAIZMC\" value=\"\"></input> <span>An extremely small bin width only plots outliers; an extremely large bin width only plots the median.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_TAUPBAIZMC\" value=\"\"></input> <span>ggplot2 automatically rejects bin widths smaller than 1.0 with a fatal error.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_TAUPBAIZMC\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>An extremely small bin width creates too much visual noise (individual spikes), while an extremely large bin width over-smooths the data and hides features like bimodality or skewness.</span></label></div>\n16.  When we want to deal with outliers, it is better to apply \"filter(...)\" so that we can isolate (and only work with) data without outliers, rather than using the \"mutate(...)\" function to change these outlier values to NA instead. <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_LRHEYQAEER'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_LRHEYQAEER\" value=\"\"></input> <span>False</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_LRHEYQAEER\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>True</span></label></div>\n17.  When you plot with NA values, how do they appear in the plots? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_MPQGMFZDYB'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_MPQGMFZDYB\" value=\"\"></input> <span>They show up as -999</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_MPQGMFZDYB\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>They are not visible at all</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_MPQGMFZDYB\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The NA values are outliers, and tidyverse has automatically removed them from the data set.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_MPQGMFZDYB\" value=\"\"></input> <span>They show up as zero, 0</span></label></div>\n18.  What is variation in the context of EDA? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_HIEIDOFLXY'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_HIEIDOFLXY\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The tendency of two variables to move together</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_HIEIDOFLXY\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The number of bins used in a histogram</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_HIEIDOFLXY\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>The tendency of a single variable&apos;s values differ from one measurement to the next</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_HIEIDOFLXY\" value=\"\"></input> <span>A coding error that produces incorrect plots</span></label></div>\n19.  A violin plot shows two distinct humps for one group. What does a boxplot of the same group most likely fail to reveal? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_XCJAYXHNWD'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_XCJAYXHNWD\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>That the group actually has two common value ranges rather than one smooth distribution </span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_XCJAYXHNWD\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Whether outliers exist</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_XCJAYXHNWD\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The group&apos;s median value</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_XCJAYXHNWD\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The exact sample size of the group </span></label></div>\n20.  You track two things over a month: (1) how many hours you sleep each night, which bounces around from 5 to 9 depending on the day, and (2) on days you drink more cups of coffee after 3pm, you tend to sleep noticeably fewer hours that night. Which of these is variation, and which is covariation? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_NDQWHUUGDO'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_NDQWHUUGDO\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Both are examples of covariation</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_NDQWHUUGDO\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Both are examples of variation</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_NDQWHUUGDO\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>Sleep hours night to night = variation; cups of afternoon coffee vs. hours slept = covariation</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_NDQWHUUGDO\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Sleep hours night to night = covariation; cups of afternoon coffee vs. hours slept = variation</span></label></div>\n21.  Two plots show the same scatterplot of `hwy` against `displ` with a `geom_smooth()` line. Plot A adds `coord_cartesian(xlim = c(5, 6))` and Plot B adds `scale_x_continuous(limits = c(5, 6))`. Which statement is true? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_JERZONXGHB'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JERZONXGHB\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Both fit the smooth line using only the points inside the window.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JERZONXGHB\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Neither changes the smooth line.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JERZONXGHB\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>Plot A fits the smooth line using all of the data and then crops the view, while Plot B fits it using only the points inside the window.</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JERZONXGHB\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Plot A fits the smooth line using only the points inside the window, while Plot B fits it using all of the data.</span></label></div>\n:::\n\n\n\n22. Consider the following three charts, all of which use the exact same data but with different aesthetic mappings and/or faceting. The data shown are from the `gapminder` data set, which includes 185 different countries, separated into Economic Regions and Continents. The plots show the GDP per capita for each country in 1972 and 2002, where data from countries within a region / continent are summarised together into box plots. \n    a. [3 marks] For each plot, give at least one interpretation that cannot be discerned from the other plots.\n    b. [2 marks] Which plot best answers the question \"Has GDP per Capita increased between 1972 and 2002?\" Justify your answer.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](MT1_Practice_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-6-1.png){width=960}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](MT1_Practice_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-7-1.png){width=960}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](MT1_Practice_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-8-1.png){width=960}\n:::\n:::\n",
